{"data": {"id": 163506, "accession_number": "2004.65", "share_license_status": "CC0", "tombstone": "Four Poems: Calligraphy in Cursive Script (xingshu), 1500s-1600s. Chen Jiru (Chinese, 1558–1639). Handscroll; ink on gold speckled paper; calligraphy section: 27 x 195 cm (10 5/8 x 76 3/4 in.). The Cleveland Museum of Art, Gift of Dr. Roger Y.K. and Mrs. Evangeline C. Hsu, Dr. Daphne T. Hsu, and Dr. Jeffrey T. Hsu in honor of Dr. Ju-hsi Chou, 2004.65", "current_location": null, "title": "Four Poems: Calligraphy in Cursive Script (xingshu)", "creation_date": "1500s-1600s", "creation_date_earliest": 1558, "creation_date_latest": 1639, "artists_tags": ["male"], "culture": ["China, Ming dynasty (1368–1644)"], "technique": "handscroll; ink on gold speckled paper", "support_materials": [], "department": "Chinese Art", "collection": "ASIAN - Handscroll", "type": "Calligraphy", "measurements": "Calligraphy section: 27 x 195 cm (10 5/8 x 76 3/4 in.)", "dimensions": {"overall": {"height": 0.33, "height_inch": 13, "height_inch_fraction": 0.0, "width": 7.944, "width_inch": 312, "width_inch_fraction": 0.75}, "calligraphy section": {"height": 0.27, "height_inch": 10, "height_inch_fraction": 0.625, "width": 1.95, "width_inch": 76, "width_inch_fraction": 0.75}}, "state_of_the_work": null, "edition_of_the_work": null, "copyright": null, "inscriptions": [{"inscription": null, "inscription_translation": "\"Immortals Playing Chess\"\n\nGreen hair grows on the red terrace,\nThe cottage stands by the ten thousand pines.\nThe immortal guests have not exchanged their names. \nPlaying chess,\nThey do not know each other. As the clouds come,\nThe chess board is darkened; As the clouds go,\nBlack and white appears.\nOnly the white-bearded ape watches by the side, \nWithout any sign of fear.", "inscription_remark": null, "sortorder": null}, {"inscription": null, "inscription_translation": "\"Fishermen\"\n\nWent fishing yesterday; going fishing today. \nYesterday the fishing was non-stop, \nSearching out fish on a new shore.\nToday fishing by the river is leisurely. \nSelling the fish,\nFor a little cash.\nA seven-feet fishing line and a half-slanting rod. \nThe old man is as busy as usual.\nThrowing away success, \nWith the fishing rod\nHe sits in solitude in the depth of nature.", "inscription_remark": null, "sortorder": null}, {"inscription": null, "inscription_translation": "\"Composed while Escaping the Heat\"\n\nMy humble body is resting under the tree.\nA guest is passing through the eastern pavilion. \nMy robe is dry;\nThe collar tinted with dew.\nHow priceless to appreciate the pine-sweeping breeze! \nStream herbs grow in the direction of the mountains.\nAnnotating the Classics\nTo express gratitude to the ancient men; \nTraveling no farther than a mile\nTo become neighbors\nAnd to view the valley in the east.", "inscription_remark": null, "sortorder": null}, {"inscription": null, "inscription_translation": "\"Composed in a Dream\"\n\nThe immortals are ashamed of worldly pleasures. \nThey live in recluse to attain happiness.\nDreams pass among the clouds,\nAnd when clouds pass, chill fills one’s dreams. \nAll the birds go in pairs,\nThere isn’t a mountain without yellow flowers. \nAttaining sudden enlightenment,\nBut do not forget one’s origins.", "inscription_remark": null, "sortorder": null}], "exhibitions": {"current": [{"id": 312307, "title": "Power and Possession: Chinese Calligraphy and Inscribed Objects – Chinese Gallery Rotation 240a, 241c", "description": "<i>Power and Possession: Chinese Calligraphy and Inscribed Objects – Chinese Gallery Rotation 240a, 241c</i>. The Cleveland Museum of Art (organizer) (August 13, 2018-February 3, 2019).", "opening_date": "2018-08-13T04:00:00"}], "legacy": [{"description": "<em>Main Asian Rotation (Gallery 242)</em>. The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (July 28, 2014-January 7, 2015).", "opening_date": "2014-07-28T00:00:00"}]}, "provenance": [{"description": "Dr. Roger Y. K. Hsu [1927–2022] and Mrs. Evangeline C. Hsu, Dr. Daphne T. Hsu, and Dr. Jeffrey T. Hsu, given to the Cleveland Museum of Art", "citations": [], "footnotes": ["<div><!--block-->Dr. Roger Y. K. and Mrs. Eva Hsu are the parents of Drs. Daphne T. Hsu and Jeffrey T. Hsu.</div>"], "date": "?–2004", "sortorder": 1}, {"description": "The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH", "citations": [], "footnotes": [], "date": "2004–", "sortorder": 2}], "find_spot": null, "related_works": [], "former_accession_numbers": ["60.2001"], "did_you_know": null, "early_education_description": null, "artlens_description": null, "description": "Written in graceful calligraphy with well-composed characters, the artist communicates his thoughts and state of mind in four poems. Characters written in dark, thick strokes convey rhythm, and indicate the moment after the writer had paused to recharge his brush with ink. Chen Jiru was a native of Huating (modern Shanghai). His calligraphy style resembles that of his friend, influential calligrapher Dong Qichang (1555–1636). Unlike Dong, Chen did not pursue government service: he burned his Confucian robes, declined a summons to serve at court, and immersed himself in literature, calligraphy, and painting.", "external_resources": {"wikidata": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60755045"], "internet_archive": ["https://archive.org/details/clevelandart-2004.65-four-poems-calligrap"]}, "citations": [], "url": "https://clevelandart.org/art/2004.65", "images": {"annotation": null, "web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_web.jpg", "width": "900", "height": "136", "filesize": "130923", "filename": "2004.65_web.jpg"}, "print": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_print.jpg", "width": "3400", "height": "514", "filesize": "729557", "filename": "2004.65_print.jpg"}, "full": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_full.tif", "width": "16218", "height": "2454", "filesize": "119419304", "filename": "2004.65_full.tif"}}, "alternate_images": [{"web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt0_web.jpg", "width": "900", "height": "73", "filesize": "53977"}, "print": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt0_print.jpg", "width": "3400", "height": "274", "filesize": "293976"}, "full": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt0_full.tif", "width": "73114", "height": "5888", "filesize": "1291520432"}, "date_created": "2014-07-11T14:26:36", "annotation": ""}, {"web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt1_web.jpg", "width": "900", "height": "727", "filesize": "224273"}, "print": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt1_print.jpg", "width": "3400", "height": "2746", "filesize": "3433080"}, "full": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt1_full.tif", "width": "7428", "height": "6000", "filesize": "133735476"}, "date_created": "2014-07-11T14:26:36", "annotation": ""}, {"web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt2_web.jpg", "width": "900", "height": "675", "filesize": "175323"}, "print": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt2_print.jpg", "width": "3400", "height": "2550", "filesize": "2981919"}, "full": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt2_full.tif", "width": "8000", "height": "6000", "filesize": "144030068"}, "date_created": "2014-07-11T14:33:48", "annotation": ""}, {"web": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt3_web.jpg", "width": "900", "height": "675", "filesize": "128025"}, "print": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt3_print.jpg", "width": "3400", "height": "2550", "filesize": "2224336"}, "full": {"url": "https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/2004.65/2004.65_alt3_full.tif", "width": "8000", "height": "6000", "filesize": "144029468"}, "date_created": 